> mapred=list(mapred.map.tasks=1000)) and in the map compute the line
> corresponding to i.
>
> (setting mapped.map.tasks=N, is not such a good idea if the time taken
> to compute for N is smaller than the time taken
> to start the map task, this is true for many  task distributers).
>
> On another note, is Hbase a viable option? I might have an option to
> (at least) read from Hbase. then you're do as above
> and for key=i (i-1...N), read the i'th row from an Hbase table.

Hi,

I really appreciate your help.
But I'm having a hard time wrapping my head around these concepts.

Let me try and simplify the problem. Let's say I have single input file like this:

row1
row2
...
row200000

and I just want to produce output like this:

key: row1
value: <some random number>
key: row2
value: <some random number>
...
key: row200000
value: <some random number>

There is only one step to the computation (so there's a map step but no reducer, or vice versa if that is an option), and let's say that the computation on each row takes a significant amount of time, and we're concerned with speed, so we want to make this as parallel as possible.
For our purposes, the random number can be generated with runif(1).

What would be the Rhipe code that would do this?

As  background, I should note that I am not just trying to solve the problem that I originally posted about (though that is an important test case), what I would like to do is create a framework for solving this and similar user-defined problems in a fast, interactive way. The idea is that a user could easily compute a single row on their own laptop, but when there are 1000 rows, it becomes prohibitively slow. If instead the user had an R session on a cluster with 1000 cores, they could compute 1000 rows as quickly as they could solve for one on their laptop.  MPI becomes fragile and unreliable with a cluster of this size, but Rhipe intrigues me because Hadoop is designed to be robust to nodes failing, etc.
I want to hide all the Rhipe/Hadoop details from the user, and just take a function that the user defines, which can compute a single row.
So, being able to solve a silly problem like the one posed above is important to my understanding of how to make such a framework.

Thanks much,
Dan

>
> Cheers
> Saptarshi

