Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

So, if you download latest rhipe 69 from github [1], the following code will take an input file
and each row can be a parallel task

simulator <- function(inputfile,Numsplits){
  list(mapreduce.input.lineinputformat.linespermap=Numsplits,
       parse.ifolder=FALSE,
       rhipe_input_folder=inputfile,
       rhipe_inputformat_class="org.godhuli.rhipe.RNLineInputFormat",
       rhipe_inputformat_keyclass="org.godhuli.rhipe.RHNumeric",
       rhipe_inputformat_valueclass="org.godhuli.rhipe.RHText")
}

m <- rhmap({
  'r' is a string corresponding to a row
  rhcollect(k, F(r))
})

results <- rhwatch(rhmr(map=m,mapred=simulator("/user/sguha/tmp/testfile",5),param=list(F=F)))

Here testfile has 24 rows, simulator creates a job with 5 parallel splits each of ~4 elements each.
F is a user defined function.

Note: when you have 200K splits and you want to read all quickly from the HDFS (remember we're reading 200K key-value pairs)
there are smater ways to read.

[1] https://github.com/downloads/saptarshiguha/RHIPE/Rhipe_0.69.tar.gz

On Wed, Jul 11, 2012 at 9:13 PM, Saptarshi Guha <saptars...@gmail.com> wrote:
> -----------------

>
> row1
> row2
> ...
> row200000
>
> and I just want to produce output like this:
>
> key: row1
> value: <some random number>
> key: row2
> value: <some random number>
> ...
> key: row200000
> value: <some random number>
>
> There is only one step to the computation (so there's a map step but no reducer, or vice versa if that is an option), and let's say that the computation on each row takes a significant amount of time, and we're concerned with speed, so we want to make this as parallel as possible.
> For our purposes, the random number can be generated with runif(1).
>
> What would be the Rhipe code that would do this?

> -------------------
>
>
> With the current setup (and this /can/ be modified) if you have one file with 200K rows, then it is almost sure that they will be run sequentially.
> This is because Hadoop splits the file at the byte level, typically 64MB being the smallest. I'm positive that the 200K row file will be at most 64MB,
> hence the entire file will be allocated to one compute node.
>
> This can be modified by customizing an inputformat, if this what is really needed, I can have one done by tomorrow EOD (fairly standard code
> exists (see NLineInputFormat[1] which sends one line as a chunk to compute on). This is not in RHIPE, but can easily be added - I'll show how in a follwup email.
>
> Lets assume this is done, then we would have (something like)
>
> map     = rhmap({ code that runs on a line})
> mapred  = list( mapred.input.format=NLineInputFormat,numlines.per.compute = 10) ...
> results = rhwatch(rhmr(map=map, reduce=0, ifolder=inputfile,mapred=mapred))
>
> this would haev 200K/10 parallel tasks, each task doing 10 computes in sequence. Hopefully, the time taken to do 10 much outweights any startup cost.
>
> Now if the data is in memory, then we can do this right now
>
> convertToList <- function(df){
>   rlo <- rownames(df)
>   if(is.null(rlo)) rlo <- 1:nrow(df)
>   lapply(1:nrow(df),function(i) list(rlo[i], df[i,]))
> }
> N <- 200000
> x <- data.frame(a=rep(1,N),b=rep(2,N),c=rep(3,N))
>
> rhwrite(convertToList(x),dest="/user/sguha/tmp/sims2",N=20000))
> map     = rhmap({ code that runs on a line})
> results = rhwatch(rhmr(map=map, reduce=0, ifolder="/user/sguha/tmp/sims2"))
>
> But i would not recommend this: 20K small files in a directory doesn't keep Hadoop happy.
>
> If you would like to work on this, I can create an inputformat for your case (i.e. given a file on N lines, decompose it into parallel splits of n lines)
>
> Regards
> Saptarshi
>
